WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.900
On behalf of the propmasters who worked on
one of the greatest science fiction films

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.750
of all time: uhh… whoops?

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:11.839
Lucasfilm executive Pablo Hidalgo recently
took to Twitter to share an image which perhaps

00:00:11.839 --> 00:00:16.410
constitutes, considering Star Wars' popularity,
one of the biggest goofs in the history of

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:17.410
cinema.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:21.410
We'll put it this way: if you never knew that
the Skywalker clan originated in the Big Apple,

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:22.960
you're in for a surprise.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.790
We know what you're thinking…

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:25.980
"I don't follow."

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.400
But bear with us.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.380
In Hidalgo's tweet, the executive began by
noting his belief that the Star Wars flicks

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.820
offer something new to the audience upon every
viewing, an assessment that probably has more

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.940
to do with the series' diligent attention
to detail than it does boneheaded errors.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.319
But upon his latest viewing of Empire, Hidalgo
had one small detail leap out at him like

00:00:44.319 --> 00:00:49.200
a vicious Wampa defending its territory: the
etchings on the bottom of Luke Skywalker's

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.200
lightsaber.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.859
Remember the iconic scene in which the budding
Jedi uses the Force to summon his weapon and

00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:56.809
free himself from his icy, upside-down imprisonment?

00:00:56.809 --> 00:00:58.600
It's pretty cool fantasy stuff, right?

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.920
But it turns out, this moment is much more
grounded in reality than you might expect.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:03.920
Hidalgo wrote,

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.780
"I've noted this before, but every time I
watch a Star Wars movie, there's something

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:07.780
new to notice.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.470
Especially if I'm fortunate to catch it on
the big screen.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.410
This latest discovery? that 'NEW YORK' is
visible on the bottom of Luke's lightsaber

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:15.410
in EMPIRE."

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:19.330
It certainly seems like a heck of an oversight
on the part of the props department.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.660
So how did it happen?

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:24.620
In standard theatrical screenings, the markings
would have been barely legible to the audience.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:30.160
But The Empire Strikes Back was a hotly anticipated
sequel to the single most pop culture-dominating,

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.720
visual effects-driven blockbuster the world
had ever seen.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.970
As a result, it enjoyed a profitable run in
theaters in the high-definition 70mm format,

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.820
which makes the markings much more visible.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.910
Still, it seems no one noticed or made note
of the goof until 2019.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.620
How could it be that out of thousands of fans,
viewing this scene in the highest resolution

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:51.710
available, not one of them noticed that Luke's
lightsaber was apparently manufactured in

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:52.710
New York?

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.980
For that matter, we'll do you one better.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.890
Empire has been available on Blu-Ray for years,
a release which boasts pristine resolution

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.750
and has sold tons of copies - probably literally.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.730
And still, no one noticed the error.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:08.369
Many Star Wars aficionados consider The Empire
Strikes Back to be the undisputed high point

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:12.520
of the entire series; there are probably some
fans who have watched this movie more times

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.140
than the sum total of every movie you have
ever seen.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.110
So the fact that this blatant mistake has
escaped the attention of one of the most dedicated

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.920
and rabid fan bases on the planet for almost
four decades is, quite simply, mind-boggling.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.160
"Are your minds blown?"

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.240
Hidalgo engaged in a lively exchange with
fans in the wake of his post, many of whom

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:34.360
were quick to point out that while the error
was indeed visible, it wasn't exactly legible.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:35.380
Hidalgo responded,

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.920
"On the big screen it can be seen; though
of course, it took about 40 years for me to

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:40.920
notice."

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.760
Hidalgo also pointed fans who may be interested
in a deep dive into the Star Wars series'

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.209
props toward a website, partsofsw.com, that
features a nifty compendium of all of the

00:02:50.209 --> 00:02:53.790
common, everyday objects which were used to
bring the series' world to life.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:58.480
In the tweet in question, Hidalgo referred
to many of Star Wars' props as being, quote,

00:02:58.480 --> 00:02:59.480
"eco-friendly."

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.261
From looking at the collection of recycled
gun parts, tape, and old TV equipment, it

00:03:03.261 --> 00:03:06.540
seems like "eco-friendly" could just be a
synonym for"cheap."

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.430
This serves as an intriguing reminder that,
for all their unprecedented scale, the original

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:14.660
Star Wars trilogy was completely unafraid
to employ guerrilla filmmaking techniques

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.870
in service of its galaxy-spanning vision.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:18.400
That's pretty rad, if you ask us.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.849
On another wholesome note, when you think
about it, it's actually a testament to the

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:25.370
skill of the filmmakers that the enormous
worldwide audience for The Empire Strikes

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:29.410
Back was so utterly sucked into its world
that every last one of them failed to catch

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.000
this glaring detail for so long.

